@@s014 Python for the exercises
@@s015 The Python needed in the notebooks of the course, built on the doubts you had in the first weeks. Every error message here is real: I obtained it by running the code (Python 3.14, NumPy 2.4).
@@s016 Starting a notebook in VS Code
@@s017 You asked: how do I start a Jupyter session from VS Code?
@@s018 In VS Code install the extensions <b>Python</b> and <b>Jupyter</b> (they are by Microsoft).
@@s019 <b>File → Open Folder</b> and open the folder of the week, for example <code>IML2026/vscode/03-week/W02</code>. Better the whole folder than the file alone: this way the notebook sees the other files (data, <code>util_poses.py</code>).
@@s020 Open the <code>.ipynb</code> file.
@@s021 At the top right: <b>Select Kernel</b> → <b>Python Environments</b> → choose the one of Anaconda (<code>anaconda3</code>), which already has NumPy, Matplotlib and seaborn.
@@s022 Run a cell with <b>Shift + Enter</b>. The cells are run in order: if you reopen the notebook, run again from the beginning (the <code>import</code> statements are in the first cell).
@@s023 From VS Code to a cell that has run
@@s024 ModuleNotFoundError: util_poses
@@s025 <code>util_poses</code> is not an installed library: it is a file, <code>util_poses.py</code>, that the course puts in the folder of week 2. Python looks for it in the <b>working folder</b> of the notebook. If the notebook runs from another folder, it does not find it.
@@s026 Check: <code>import os; print(os.getcwd())</code> must print the folder that contains <code>util_poses.py</code>. Remedy: open the notebook that is in that folder, or before the import add <code>import sys; sys.path.append(r"path\of\the\folder\W02")</code>. (Tried with a test module: outside the folder it gives the same error, with <code>sys.path.append</code> it imports.)
@@s027 Where Python looks for util_poses
@@s028 Reading an element: the indices
@@s029 You asked: how do I read the array at a position?
@@s030 <code>a[i]</code> reads the element in position i, and one counts <b>from 0</b>. With <code>a = [2, 2, 5]</code>: <code>a[0]</code> is 2, <code>a[2]</code> is 5, <code>a[-1]</code> is the last (5). In a NumPy matrix: <code>M[i, j]</code> = row i, column j. Slices and columns are in the page <a href="w01-indicizzazione-numpy.html">Indexing NumPy arrays</a>.
@@s031 a[0], a[2], a[−1]
@@s032 The inner product written by hand
@@s033 The exercise of week 1: writing <code>dot(a, b)</code> without NumPy. Here are all the pieces you had asked about: comparing two lengths, a counter inside the <code>for</code>, reading two lists together.
@@s034 dot(a, b): the right version (verified: 14 and 11)
@@s035 <code>def</code> defines a function. <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are the <b>parameters</b>: names for the vectors that will be passed to them. The <code>:</code> open the block; the whole body is indented by 4 spaces.
@@s036 <code>len</code> counts the elements, <code>!=</code> means "different". If the lengths do not coincide the inner product is not defined, and <code>raise</code> stops everything with an error.
@@s037 The <b>accumulator</b> starts from 0: adding 0 changes nothing.
@@s038 <code>zip(a, b)</code> pairs the elements in the same position: (2, 3), then (2, 4). <code>x, y</code> unpacks each pair into two names.
@@s039 <code>somma += x * y</code> means <code>somma = somma + x * y</code>. It is the counter you had asked about: with <code>conta += 1</code> you add 1 at each turn.
@@s040 <code>return</code> gives back the result. Without <code>return</code>, the function returns <code>None</code>.
@@s041 The second test counts: it uses vectors <b>different</b> from va and vb. 1·3 + 2·4 = 11.
@@s042 What happens inside, turn by turn, for dot([2, 2], [3, 4])
@@s043 Before the loop.
@@s044 zip has given the first pair.
@@s045 Second pair. The pairs are finished, the loop stops.
@@s046 It is 2·3 + 2·4, the definition of the inner product.
@@s047 zip and the accumulator, drawn
@@s048 Three ways of running through two lists
@@s049 <code>for x, y in zip(a, b)</code>: the pairs (the most readable). <code>for i in range(len(a))</code> and then <code>a[i] * b[i]</code>: with the index. <code>for i, x in enumerate(a)</code>: index and value together (<code>enumerate(['a', 'b'])</code> gives (0, 'a'), (1, 'b')).
@@s050 The errors that have happened
@@s051 It is the version that is now in your notebook of week 1 (<code>jupyterlab/03-week/W01/02-practical_introduction.ipynb</code>; I only read it). Two problems: after the <code>for</code> the <code>:</code> are needed and the body goes on a new line, indented. And <code>+=</code> is a statement, it cannot be put inside brackets.
@@s052 The version from the chat: no error message, but the result is <b>wrong</b> (it should have been 11). Inside the function it uses <code>va</code> and <code>vb</code>, the variables from outside, instead of the parameters <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>: whatever you pass it, it always computes va · vb. The test <code>dot(va, vb) == 14</code> passes by chance. It is corrected with <code>zip(a, b)</code> and new names in the loop (<code>x, y</code>).
@@s053 Every <code>for</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>def</code> ends with the colon.
@@s054 <code>=+ 1</code> is read <code>= +1</code>: it assigns 1 at each turn. The counter is <code>+=</code> (first the plus, then the equals). With <code>+=</code> at the end it is worth 2.
@@s055 The function that uses the variables from outside
@@s056 Tuples: ms = 4, 6, 7
@@s057 You had written: ms = 4,6,7 "so I create 2 arrays containing those values"
@@s058 The commas without brackets create a <b>tuple</b> of 3 numbers: <code>(4, 6, 7)</code>. They are not pairs. To run through <b>pairs</b> of poses one needs a list of pairs.
@@s059 The <code>for</code> takes one element at a time: first the 4. <code>i, j</code> tries to split the 4 into two values, and a number does not split. Right: <code>ms = [(0, 1), (2, 3)]</code>: each element is a pair, and <code>i, j</code> unpacks it.
@@s060 I write
@@s061 I get
@@s062 Can it be changed?
@@s063 list
@@s064 yes: <code>l[0] = 5</code>, <code>l.append(8)</code>
@@s065 <code>(4, 6, 7)</code> or <code>4, 6, 7</code>
@@s066 tuple
@@s067 no
@@s068 list of pairs
@@s069 yes; each pair no
@@s070 <code>np.array([4, 6, 7])</code>
@@s071 NumPy array
@@s072 yes, and it does calculations element by element
@@s073 A tuple of numbers against a list of pairs
@@s074 The matrix of the distances and the heatmap
@@s075 Exercise of the poses (week 2, Tasks 3 and 5): the distance between <b>all</b> the pairs of poses, in an 18 × 18 matrix called <code>res</code>.
@@s076 Building res, drawing it, finding the nearest pairs (tried on 18 × 50 data)
@@s077 A matrix of zeros already of the right shape, to be filled cell by cell.
@@s078 Two loops one inside the other: for each pair (i, j) the <a href="w02-norme-distanze.html">distance</a> between the two poses goes in the cell i, j. The diagonal is 0 (a pose with itself) and the matrix is symmetric.
@@s079 <code>np.triu</code> keeps the upper triangle (diagonal included) of a <b>matrix</b>. With <code>mask</code> the heatmap hides the cells at 1: only the lower triangle remains, because the other half repeats the same numbers.
@@s080 <code>triu_indices(18, k=1)</code> gives the rows and columns of the cells <b>above</b> the diagonal: each pair once, 153 pairs. <code>argsort</code> gives the order from the smallest distance: the first are the most similar pairs, the last the most different.
@@s081 It was your error of 2 September. The message is not clear, but the cause is simple: <code>res</code> was <b>a single number</b> (a distance), that is a 0-dimensional array. <code>np.triu</code> needs a matrix from which to take rows and columns. First one builds the 18 × 18 with the two loops, then the mask.
@@s082 Python runs the cells in order and remembers only what it has already run. The cell of Task 4 uses <code>D</code>, but <code>D</code> exists only after the cell that creates it has completed successfully. The typical causes: that cell has not been run; it stopped with an error (for example <code>No module named cv2</code>, that is wrong kernel); the kernel was restarted and then only the cells at the bottom were run. The cure: <b>Kernel → Restart Kernel and Run All Cells</b>, and look at the <i>first</i> red error from the top: the others are a consequence.
@@s083 res in small: 4 points
@@s084 Classes: the <code>...</code> under a method
@@s085 A <b>class</b> groups data and functions. A function inside a class is called a <b>method</b> and always receives <code>self</code> as the first parameter: it is the object itself, so <code>self.k</code> reads or writes a datum of the object.
@@s086 In the gaze the class <code>GazeModel</code> is a <b>base</b>: it declares the methods <code>calibrate</code> and <code>predict</code> without writing them. <code>LinearGaze</code> <b>extends</b> it (<code>class LinearGaze(GazeModel)</code>) and really writes them. The advantage: <code>predict_many</code> is written only once in the base and calls <code>self.predict</code>, so it works for every model that extends it.
@@s087 The <code>...</code> (in English <i>Ellipsis</i>) is a <b>placeholder</b>: Python accepts it as a statement that does nothing. The body of the method is not written, so the function returns <code>None</code>.
@@s088 base with placeholder, daughter that completes it (verified)
@@s089 Warning: the signature does not necessarily coincide
@@s090 In the notebook the base has <code>calibrate(self, dataset)</code> and the daughter <code>calibrate(self, dataset, pupil_training_set, screen_training_set)</code>. Python does not check: the daughter replaces the base and uses its own signature. If you call a method with arguments that the class does not have, the error appears only at runtime.
